Answer: The pageNumber field is private. The setter stores the value only when it is positive. The getter returns the stored value.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
class Book {
private int pageNumber;
public void setPageNumber(int value) {
if (value > 0) {
pageNumber = value;
```

} else {

```java
System.out.println("Invalid page number");
}
}
public int getPageNumber() {
return pageNumber;
}
}
public class BookApp {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
Book book = new Book();
int value = sc.nextInt();
book.setPageNumber(value);
System.out.println("Page number: " + book.getPageNumber());
sc.close();
}
}
```

Sample Input: 100 Sample Output: Page number: 100 For an input such as -100, the setter prints Invalid page number and the getter returns the unchanged default value 0.

Answer: The Customer class keeps name and amount private. setName() accepts a name longer than three characters, setAmount() accepts an amount greater than zero, and the getter methods return the stored values.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
class Customer {
private String name;
private int amount;
public void setName(String value) {
if (value.length() > 3) {
name = value;
```

} else {

```java
System.out.println("Invalid name");
}
}
public void setAmount(int value) {
if (value > 0) {
amount = value;
```

} else {

```java
System.out.println("Invalid amount");
}
}
public String getName() {
return name;
}
public int getAmount() {
return amount;
}
}
public class CustomerApp {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
Customer customer = new Customer();
String name = sc.nextLine();
int amount = sc.nextInt();
customer.setName(name);
customer.setAmount(amount);
System.out.println("Name: " + customer.getName());
System.out.println("Amount: " + customer.getAmount());
sc.close();
}
}
```

